# CRITICAL: This is an EXAMPLE file
#
# TO USE:
# 1. Copy this file: cp .kamal/secrets-example .kamal/secrets
# 2. Fill in your actual credentials in .kamal/secrets
# 3. NEVER commit .kamal/secrets to git (already in .gitignore)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Docker Registry Authentication
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Option 1: Docker Hub
# Get your password from: https://hub.docker.com/settings/security
# Create an access token (recommended) instead of using your password
#
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=your_docker_hub_token_here

# Option 2: GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io)
# Get your token from: https://github.com/settings/tokens
# Needs: write:packages, read:packages, delete:packages permissions
#
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=your_github_token_here

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Application Secrets (if needed in future)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database (if you add PostgreSQL accessory)
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=your_secure_database_password_here

# Supabase (if using cloud Supabase instead of local PostgreSQL)
# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=your_supabase_service_role_key

# Redis password (if you enable Redis with authentication)
# REDIS_PASSWORD=your_redis_password_here

# API Keys (add as needed)
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# SENTRY_DSN=https://...

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Security Best Practices:
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ✅ Use access tokens, not passwords
# ✅ Rotate secrets regularly (every 90 days)
# ✅ Use different secrets for staging vs production
# ✅ Never commit this file after filling it in
# ✅ Store backup in secure password manager (1Password, Bitwarden)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

# How Kamal reads secrets:
# - Variables are evaluated when kamal deploy runs
# - Can use shell commands: $(cat file.txt)
# - Can use environment variables: $MY_VAR
# - Can use hardcoded values (less secure but acceptable for non-sensitive data)
